  "text": "down. Slow winds here, because most falls happen in the half second after you decide you are safe. Flat 3B is empty. On the fridge is a list, one line per day, in pen. Day 1 to day 17. The last line says, they always go for the lit ones. Under it, a drawing of a window with a cross through it. Tins, rice, a sleeping bag. 17 more days of food, 32 in total, and a feeling that the writer never left. Day Day 26. The Test. Rule one is a theory, and theories get people killed. So at dusk, you test it. You stand in the balcony doorway and bang a saucepan with a spoon. Forty seconds of racket. Not one head turns. Then you cup a lighter in both hands and flick it for a single second. A flame the size of a thumbnail. Every face in the courtyard lifts toward you at once. Eyes that have adapted to the dark can pick out even a tiny flame from far away. You were were right. You were also just seen. The balcony is finished after sunset. Sound is safe. Light is the only rule that matters. You write it inside the cupboard door, in capitals. Day 31. The log. The woman in the striped scarf vanishes for three days. You signal at noon. Three fingers, a pause, three fingers. The gap in her curtain stays black. Long isolation wears down judgment, and most people alone for weeks lose count of the date so you keep a log date water food weather one line each it will not feed you it will tell you whether you are getting worse on day 34 the hand comes back two fingers a wave you did not know a wave could weigh that much you hold up three then a pause then three again she holds up the same after that nothing happens for 10 days that is the worst part day 45 the message you count seven days of food and 75 gallons of water then at 615 in the evening to the minute a window lights up across the courtyard then the next then the next floor by floor until the whole face of the building glows then it begins to blink three short three long three short it is the oldest distress call there is and you do not need training to read it Somebody over there is begging to be found. Down in the courtyard, every head turns up. They do not read it. They do not need to. They only follow the light. In 40 seconds, your stairwell empties for the first time in 31 days. The way out is clear. Rule one says, stay dark. Then, behind you, a switch clicks. The one you taped on day one. Every window in the courtyard is lit. And the last one is yours.",
